<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carol II of Romania</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1893">1893</a>–<a href="/years/1953">1953</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carol II was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> from 1930 to 1940. Having renounced his rights to the throne in 1925, he returned in 1930 to displace his young son Michael, and in 1938 he abolished the political parties and set up a royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>. After Romania lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukovina">Bukovina</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-dobruja">Southern Dobruja</a> in 1940, he was forced to abdicate in favour of Michael and went into exile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carol-ii-of-romania.jpg" alt="Carol II of Romania" width="320" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carol_II_of_Romania.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Carol was born in Peleș Castle as the son of Crown Prince Ferdinand and Crown Princess Marie, the first male member of the royal family to be born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. He spent much of his childhood under his domineering great-uncle, King Carol I, who kept his parents out of his upbringing. His marriage to Zizi Lambrino, who was not of royal lineage, was dissolved, and in March 1921 he married Princess Helen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, with whom he had a son, Michael.</p><p>His affair with Elena Lupescu ended the marriage. The National Liberal Party used it to argue that he was unfit to be king. In 1925 he renounced his rights to the throne in favour of Michael and went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> with Lupescu. Michael inherited the throne at the age of five on the death of Ferdinand in 1927, and Helen divorced Carol in 1928.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Carol returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> on 7 June 1930 in a coup engineered by the National Peasant prime minister Iuliu Maniu, deposed Michael, who was still under a regency, and was recognised as king by Parliament the next day. After the December 1937 elections no coalition could be formed. On 10 February 1938 he suspended the constitution, proclaimed martial law and set up a royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>, abolishing all parties and forming the National Renaissance Front.</p><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> began he proclaimed neutrality. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1940 Carol moved towards Nazi Germany in the hope of avoiding invasion. In June the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> demanded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukovina">Bukovina</a> by ultimatum, and Carol gave way without fighting. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/craiova">Craiova</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-dobruja">Southern Dobruja</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, and the Second Vienna Award of 30 August 1940 gave northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. Demonstrations demanded his abdication, and General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ion-antonescu">Ion Antonescu</a> forced him to abdicate on 6 September 1940 in favour of Michael.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Carol left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> by train with thirty truckloads of his fortune; the Iron Guard shot at the train at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timisoara">Timișoara</a> in a failed attempt to kill him. He went to Portugal and then to Cuernavaca in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, where from 1941 he tried to organise a Free Romania movement, which gained no recognition. He married Lupescu in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> on 3 June 1947 and died at Estoril in 1953. The historian Stanley G. Payne called him the most cynical, corrupt and power-hungry monarch of twentieth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. His remains were returned to the Curtea de Argeș monastery in 2003.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Carol II of Romania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Such regimes often suppressed fascist movements: Austrian Nazis assassinated Dollfuss, and King <b>Carol II of Romania</b> ordered the execution of the Iron Guard leader Corneliu Zelea Codreanu.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/conservatism">Conservatism · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Peleș Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. Died in Estoril, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.36,25.54&amp;year=1932" aria-label="Peleș Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5157 563 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Carol II of Romania"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5607 833h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5607 833h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.36,25.54&amp;year=1932">What was here in 1932? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
